AI-Generated Context as Writing Model
Chakrabarty, Padmakumar, and He (2022) developed an instruction-tuning approach for collaborative poetry writing with large language models, demonstrating how AI can provide diverse stylistic models and prompt learners to articulate poetic choices explicitly. Their work suggests AI systems can serve as interactive partners that help writers clarify and defend aesthetic decisions—supporting the metacognitive dimensions of creative writing (Kellogg & Whiteford, 2009).
AI as Creativity Explorer
Coenen et al. (2022) conducted empirical investigation of human-AI co-creativity in writing through their Wordcraft platform, identifying serendipitous discovery as a key affordance—unexpected AI suggestions can trigger creative insights that solitary writing might not generate (Boden, 2004). Critically, they found that interaction quality matters more than interface design: the type of human-AI exchange (collaborative vs. directive) shaped creative outcomes more than presentation format.
AI as a tutor
  By positioning large language models as AI tutors or co-pilots that can bridge the gap between creative intention and linguistic execution at precisely the moment when learners need help most. The AI then assists in generating an initial draft in English, transforming those expressed intentions into actual poetic text. This intervention occurs at the critical juncture where many learners previously hit an insurmountable wall and either gave up or never started, thereby enabling learning opportunities that traditional approaches leave inaccessible.
  Critically, this approach preserves—rather than eliminates—the pedagogical division between decision-making (which remains squarely with the learner) and execution (where AI provides pattern-based scaffolding). Students continue to exercise genuine creative agency in determining what they want to express, why it matters to them, and how they want to frame it conceptually and emotionally. This maintains the creative and cognitive engagement essential for learning while removing the execution barrier that traditionally prevented many L2 learners from accessing poetry writing as a developmental activity.
AI as Pattern-recognizer
Recent technical research (Li et al., 2025; Peeperkorn et al., 2024; Holtzman et al., 2019) reveals that temperature and top-p settings systematically shape generation characteristics. Lower temperatures generate conventional, corpus-typical outputs aligned with training data distributions; higher temperatures increase variability and novelty. However, the pedagogical implications of these technical parameters remained largely unexplored until recently—this study addresses that gap by examining how parameter configuration shapes interaction patterns in educational contexts.
Large language models demonstrate sophisticated capacity to recognize and manipulate linguistic patterns at multiple levels—phonetic, syntactic, semantic, and aesthetic. LLMs trained on vast corpora of diverse poetry internalize patterns of poetic form, sound, structure, and meaning-making without explicit instruction about meter, rhyme, or prosody (Holtzman et al., 2019). This pattern-recognition capability parallels what experimental poets like Charles Bernstein explore in his poetry collections, where conscious manipulation of sonic texture, line breaks, and syntactic disruption create meaning through form itself. AI systems can recognize these patterns and apply them generatively to novel compositions, enabling writers to experiment with forms and effects they might not have mastered through traditional study alone. Critically, AI can do this across multiple poetic dimensions simultaneously—managing rhythm while maintaining semantic coherence, manipulating sound patterns while preserving emotional resonance. This multidimensional pattern-handling capacity potentially transforms AI from a tool for generating thematic content into a sophisticated resource for exploring how form and pattern create aesthetic and emotional effects—a dimension particularly valuable for L2 learners developing sensitivity to English’s linguistic and poetic patterns.